Just that in some languages, words have a different form depending on 
whether there are exactly one, exactly two, or more than two.
Wikipedia gives this example from Slovene:
  babarija (old wives tale) (singular), 
  babariji (two old wives tales) (dual), 
  babarije (three old wives tales) (plural)
and this from Hebrew:
  yom (day) (singular), 
  yomaim (two days) (dual),
  yamim (days) (plural) 

Based on later conversation, I'm guessing ancient Greek is such a 
language too.  So Boudewijn was perhaps taking your flat joke and giving 
it a good spin.


